\
Review #8A\
===========================================================================\
\
Overall merit\
-------------\
4. Accept\
\
Reviewer expertise\
------------------\
3. Knowledgeable\
\
Paper summary\
-------------\
\strike \strikec0 The paper presents a TPTP formal for outputting connection tableaux refutation proofs.\
This is an incremental improvement on the previous work by Otten; one of the four\
formats that leanCoP can output. In particular it gives more explicit proof information.\strike0\striked0 \
\
Comments for authors\
--------------------\
The contribution is clear and it is clear that it needs to be made, but the details as\
to what it enables is not stated clearly. We see that it enables the IDV/GDV diagrams\
and proof visualisation, but what else?\
\
RELATION TO PREVIOUS WORK:\
\
I would definitely like to see an explicit comparison of the current approach versus the three approaches mentioned -- their satisfaction of the requirements is compared but a more mechanical comparison (i.e. "[16] records the results in this way, whereas [15] does this," etc.) would be better to demonstrate the material contribution of the current paper. In the first part of section 3 it states that [16] "...left some important information as implicit, which is made explicit in the new format" but never actually directly states what this information is. From what I can tell, the differences are:\
\
explicit connection steps in the proof,\
below records in lemmas,\
explicit SZS status,\
but I needed to compare the format to the format in [16] manually to find this out. \strike \strikec0 I wonder as well whether the inclusion of SZS status overlaps with the TPTP role field.\strike0\striked0 \
\
\strike \strikec0 FORMAT REQUIREMENTS:\
\
The requirements introduced in part (3) are reasonable, however requirements 4 and 5 are subjective and not measurable. I think that the presentation as "requirements" suggests objectivity. The statement that the first historical proposal "might have also failed to meet requirements 4,5" does not really have any criteria to measure against (hence, probably, "might have"). I can see how (4,5) were taken directly from [16] so really this is an issue with that paper if anything: there's no explicit justification there either for why these requirements exist, for example, citing prior work which criticises existing approaches for being overly complex or unreadable. To be clear I think it is obvious why these requirements are beneficial but it is not completely clear why they are necessary.\strike0\striked0 \
\
PRESENTATION:\
\
\strike \strikec0 There is ambiguity in the text at various points, as to whether the authors are referring to tableaux generally or connection tableaux, which is difficult to follow. Some examples of this are in section 1 paragraph 2, or section 3 paragraph 2, or the conclusion. It's hard to tell whether the authors mean that the syntax for connection proofs is new, or the syntax for tableau proofs generally. At times the authors refer to "...the TPTP format for..." without qualification which suggests the format is already established, rather than saying "the proposed format" or "the present format" or similar. At the end it is specifically stated that non-clausal tableaux are not handled - depending on how common non-clausal tableaux are, this might be worth an earlier mention.\strike0\striked0 \
\
