Review #8B\
===========================================================================\
\
Overall merit\
-------------\
4. Accept\
\
Reviewer expertise\
------------------\
3. Knowledgeable\
\
Paper summary\
-------------\
\strike \strikec0 The paper describes a new TPTP/TSTP-based format for recording closed tableaux\
proofs. A closed tableau is represented as a sequence of TPTP formulas\
annotated with inference information, similarly to the existing TPTP proof\
format for saturation-based provers. Each formula F is annotated with\
information about its parent formula P in the tableau. When the parent is a\
multi-literal clause (a disjunction), the annotation also specifies the literal\
(e.g., P:2 for the second literal) under which the formula is placed in the\
tableau. In this way, the tree-like structure of the tableau is captured in the\
proof, since disjunction naturally corresponds to tableau branching.\
Additionally, the format records semantic parents S, which logically entail F\
(S \uc0\u8658  F). This enables verification that the tableau is valid and that all\
branches are properly closed.\strike0\striked0 \
\
\strike \strikec0 The tableau is recorded in its closed form, with all closing substitutions\
applied. The format currently supports standard tableau inference rules for\
branch extension and branch closure, as well as lemma introduction from a\
closed branch and subsequent lemma application. The format can also optionally\
record substitution applications, although these are not required for the\
verification of closed tableaux.\strike0\striked0 \
\
\strike \strikec0 In addition to the format itself, the authors implement proof output for the\
connection calculus prover CONNECT++, proof verification in the tool GDV, and\
tableau proof visualization in the tool IDV. The paper provides a clear summary\
of the state of the art in TPTP proof formats, properly motivates the work, and\
describes and extends previous attempts at tableau proof formats.\
\
I believe the format to be generic enough to be useful for other provers as\
well and becoming a standard. Hence I recommend accepting the paper.\strike0\striked0 \
\
Comments for authors\
--------------------\
\strike \strikec0 1. You state that the tableau is represented in closed form; however, in Figure\
5, the left-most branch is closed using ~q(b) and q(Y). In that case, Y should\
be unified with b, and thus q(Y) should appear as q(b) (including in the lemma\
alongside ~q(b)). Alternatively, this may simply be a typo, and ~q(b) should\
instead be q(Y), as also shown in clause t2 in Figure 6. This point should be\
clarified in the paper.\strike0\striked0 \
\
\
\strike \strikec0 2. You state that semantic verification is essentially the same as for\
derivations, but is there also a check that the logical parents occur on the\
same branch leading to the leaf? For example, what would happen if\
\
cnf(t12,plain, $false,\
    inference(connection,[status(thm),parent(t11:1)],[t9:2,t11:1]) ).\
\
were instead derived from [t2:2,t11:1]? The resulting set would still be\
\{~p(c), p(c)\}. Would such a derivation pass the verification check?\strike0\striked0 \
\
\
3. Some tableau provers operate on NNF formulas rather than CNFs. Do you\
support formulas in NNF form (e.g., using fof formulas in TPTP)?\
\
