Review #8C\
===========================================================================\
\
Overall merit\
-------------\
3. Weak accept\
\
Reviewer expertise\
------------------\
3. Knowledgeable\
\
Paper summary\
-------------\
\strike \strikec0 This paper presents the new (final/standardized?) format for representing connection tableaux in TPTP. The format builds on earlier rules for representing the clausification step of provers, the new part are the rules and conventions for the tableaux themselves. The rules cover the standard extension step of (strong) connection tableaux; closure of a branch due to complementary literals; and the creation and application of unit lemmas.\strike0\striked0 \
\
Comments for authors\
--------------------\
\strike \strikec0 A mostly to-the-point presentation of a reasonable new format for tableau proofs. There are some aspects of the format that this reviewer finds a bit problematic: in particular, the tree structure of a tableau is not explicitly represented in the format (e.g., using nested parentheses), instead every expansion step introduces a new top-level "cnf" statement, even though the introduced formulas might live on some branch of the tableau only (it is not a consequence of the problem). But this might be a matter of taste, and the format should be easy to generate and to parse.\strike0\striked0 \
\
\strike \strikec0 One aspect that should be explained more clearly in the paper is the handling of substitutions. In Fig 5, it is not obvious why some atoms contain variables and some atoms are instantiated with ground terms, and similarly in Fig 6. E.g., q(Y) in [t1:1] is resolved with ~q(b) in [t2:1], should the Y therefore be replaced by b?\strike0\striked0 \
\
It is not clear to me why two rules are needed for closing proof branches ("connection" and "reduction"), wouldn't it be sufficient have only one of them? It would still be easy to check that a given proof satisfies the connection condition.\
\
While the paper is a good starting point, it is a bit disappointing that none of the natural next steps are discussed: adding equality, theories like arithmetic, etc.\
\
It should also be discussed how the format compares to the more general SC-TPTP, which could be used easily to represent connection tableaux as well.\
}